      "matching_lines": [
        {
          "line": 15,
          "text": "## 2. Printed ceramics versus micron nuclear layer"
        },
        {
          "line": 16,
          "text": "A relevant direct demonstration: *Interface engineering and bimodal gradation strategy for high-precision vat photopolymerization 3D printing of boron nitride ceramics*, *Ceramics International* 52 (2026), https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ceramint.2026.04.157. Actual technical obstacles include UV scattering, high ceramic solids-versus-viscosity tradeoffs, debinding, sintering, cracking and anisotropic shrinkage. A 2026 *Nature* DISH paper https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-026-10114-5 reports ~19-µm print resolution in photopolymers and complex mm objects in ~0.6 s, **not** sintered 4–6 µm BN foil manufacture. Acoustic levitation/control and optical cure are separate enabling fields."
        },
        {
          "line": 45,
          "text": "B. **Hybrid catcher fabrication G0-F**: compare commercial hBN, deposited boron film, patterned/graded BN ceramic on characterized lightweight support, and printed support with deposited B layer. Record ¹¹B areal density, film strength, porosity, backing and 0–10 shot drift."
        },
        {
          "line": 51,
          "text": "**Mandatory anti-overclaim statements:** Printed ≠ dense final ceramic; levitated ≠ atomically placed; SiC/diamond ≠ inherently EMP immune; tungsten divertor ≠ qualified converter grid; conference spin talk ≠ 1.5× observed yield; ¹¹C radioactive production ≠ instant/meaningful target self-enrichment; Bayesian optimization ≠ unbiased causal proof; source-traced ≠ experimentally calibrated."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "path": "research/stars_2_0/reviews/DEEPSEEK_V08_HYBRID_BORON_TARGET_FABRICATION_FOLLOWUP_2026-10-27.md",
      "url": "https://github.com/atlaslattice/open-regenerative-compute-standard/blob/c96d12176dbd5613f148f3f51df5a746f42952dd/research/stars_2_0/reviews/DEEPSEEK_V08_HYBRID_BORON_TARGET_FABRICATION_FOLLOWUP_2026-10-27.md",
      "git_blob_sha": "49041e236401e06f835de5e97a3379dc8fd8b08d",
      "matching_lines": [
        {
          "line": 13,
          "text": "3. **Supporting hardware:** Fabricated target holder, alignment assembly, membranes and microfluidic structure; potentially DISH-photopolymerized or separately ceramic printed. Their dimensions and chemistry are distinct from the active isotope-bearing reaction film."
        },
        {
          "line": 15,
          "text": "DISH's reported 11–19 µm optical printing resolution does **not** demonstrate direct printing of 4–6 µm, high-density, nearly stoichiometric BN targets. Ceramic vat photopolymerization requires separate debinding/sintering/porosity/shrinkage characterization. Acoustic levitation of liquids does not show that a solid sintered BN layer can be plastically formed by sound."
        }
      ]
